#: processes/sdlc/code-quality-gates.bpmn:14
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "EVERY JOB IS INDEPENDENT, AND NOTHING IN THE YAML SAYS SO IN ONE PLACE. Bean `7yvd`. The file reads as a long sequence; it is not one. NO job declares `needs:`, so they all start together and the wall-clock cost of the whole workflow is the SLOWEST job, not the sum. That is the single most useful thing to know before adding a gate, and it is recoverable today only by checking for `needs:` keys that are not there. COUNT THE `# bpmn-node:` DECLARATIONS rather than trusting a number here: this paragraph said SIX until 2026-09-26, by which point there were eight — `typescript` had been split into `typescript` + `gates` and `skill-registration-chain` added — and the count was wrong in the one place a reader checks the shape. THE ASYMMETRY IS THE OTHER HALF, AND IT IS THE PART THAT DOES NOT GO STALE. Exactly TWO jobs report without blocking — `rust-wildcard` and `dependency-advisories` — and every other job is HARD. Stated that way round on purpose: the warn-only set is named and closed, so it stays true as jobs are added, whereas \"four are hard\" needed editing the moment a ninth gate existed. Drawing them as equal boxes would be a lie a reader would act on, so both warn-only jobs are labelled as such and the gateway after the join asks specifically about the HARD ones. THE TWO WARN-ONLY JOBS ARE WARN-ONLY BY DIFFERENT MECHANISMS, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE POINT. `rust-wildcard` carries `continue-on-error: true` — the RUNNER discards its result. `dependency-advisories` carries no such flag: its script exits 0 in every state and keeps the three states apart in its OUTPUT instead. That is deliberate, because a warn-only step whose exit code says nothing has only its output left to distinguish `found nothing` from `could not find out`, and `continue-on-error` would have thrown away the one signal that still worked. `continue-on-error` IS NOT A DEFAULT HERE, it is a decision that has been REVERSED once already: `python-imports` carried it, and the flag swallowed a failure on a missing path, so the job reported success over a check that never ran. It was removed and a new unused import now fails. The flag STILL survives on exactly one job — adding a second warn-only job did not add a second `continue-on-error`, which is the history being honoured rather than an oversight. Anyone reaching for the flag should read that paragraph first. `TypeScript` IS ONE BOX STANDING FOR ROUGHLY THIRTY REPOSITORY GATES — workflow policy, AGENTS.md cross-references and claims, declared assets, workflow skill refs and BPMN coverage, the knowledge-graph audit, generated-docs currency, agent memory, and the ratchet that fails a check script no workflow runs. They are sequential only because one job runs them; each is independent, and `bun run gates` is the local runner derived from this job so a contributor can run the same set before pushing. Mechanical throughout. Nothing here waits on a person, which is what makes `build-pipeline` the right lane rather than a convenient one."
msgstr ""

